# The 10 Headline Formulas That Actually Get Clicks

Here's the brutal truth about content marketing: **your headline isn't just the first thing people read — it's the only thing most people will read.**

On average, **8 out of 10 people** will read your headline, but only **2 out of 10** will read past it to your actual content. That means if your headline doesn't grab attention, your carefully crafted article dies on the vine.

The good news? Great headlines aren't magic. They follow proven formulas that you can replicate — and scale — with the right tool.

---

## The 10 Headline Formulas

### 1. The Curiosity Gap

> "The Secret Reason Your AI Strategy Isn't Working"

This formula makes a bold claim and promises to reveal something the reader doesn't know yet. It doesn't give everything away — it *withholds* just enough to create tension.

**Pattern:** "The Secret/What If/How [outcome] Isn't What You Think"

### 2. The How-To

> "How to Automate Your SaaS Workflow in 10 Minutes"

Direct promise of a practical outcome. People love step-by-step instructions — it's why "how to" searches dominate Google.

**Pattern:** "How to [achieve X] in [timeframe/budget]"

### 3. The Question Hook

> "What Top SaaS Founders Know About AI That You Don't"

This hits at the reader's fear of missing out. It positions them as potentially uninformed and offers to close the gap.

**Pattern:** "What [group] Know/Knew That [you don't]"

### 4. The Listicle (with numbers)

> "5 AI Tools That Save Your Business 20+ Hours Weekly"

Numbers make content scannable and promise concrete value. The brain processes "5 items" faster than an essay.

**Pattern:** "[Number] [adjective] [noun] That [benefit]"

### 5. Breaking News

> "Breaking: This Simple Change Boosted Conversions by 342%"

Creates urgency and FOMO. The word "breaking" signals timeliness and that the reader might miss something important.

**Pattern:** "Breaking: [change] [result]"

### 6. The Command

> "Stop Sending Boring Emails — Do This Instead"

Direct and authoritative. It tells the reader exactly what to do and what NOT to do.

**Pattern:** "[Verb] [negative thing] — [positive alternative]"

### 7. Problem-Agitate-Solution (PAS)

> "Struggling With Slow Websites? Here's What It's Costing You"

First you feel the pain, then you panic a little, then you realize the article will give you relief.

**Pattern:** "[Problem]? Here's what it's costing you."

### 8. Benefit-Driven

> "Double Your Email Opens by This Friday"

Pure promise of gain. The reader immediately asks: "Really? How?"

**Pattern:** "[Benefit] by [timeframe]"

### 9. Social Proof

> "Why 837 Companies Switched to This Workflow This Year"

Leverages the herd mentality. If others are doing it, maybe you should too.

**Pattern:** "Why [number] [group] [action]"

### 10. Fear of Scarcity

> "This Automation Hack Has a 48-Hour Window — Use It Now"

Creates urgency through limited-time exclusivity. The reader must act fast or lose the opportunity.

**Pattern:** "[Hack/trick] has a [time] window — [call to action]"

---

## The Real Problem

Knowing these formulas is one thing. **Writing 8-12 variations of your headline is tedious.** And writing them *well* — with the right power words, character counts, and platform-specific hooks — takes years of practice and countless A/B tests.

Most content creators spend 30 minutes on a headline. That's 30 minutes they could spend writing the actual content.

